Now let me look for an impactful, testable improvement. Let me check the strip_ansi_codes function - it currently only handles CSI sequences (ESC [) but modern terminals output OSC sequences (ESC ]) for hyperlinks and titles. These leak through as garbage text in tool output, wasting tokens:
  ╭─ Turn 29 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
  ▶ $ cd /home/runner/work/yoyo-evolve/yoyo-evolve && sed -n '60,100p' src/format/outp… ✓ (4ms)

This is a real gap. The function only handles:
1. CSI sequences (ESC [ ... letter)

But it doesn't handle:
1. OSC sequences (ESC ] ... ST) — terminal hyperlinks, window titles (very common in modern cargo, `rustc, ls` output)
2. Two-character sequences (ESC followed by a letter like ESC M, ESC D) 
3. CSI parameter bytes — it only consumes digits/semicolons but the spec allows `?, >, <, =, !` as private marker bytes

This is impactful because leaked OSC sequences waste LLM tokens and create confusing output. Modern rustc uses OSC 8 hyperlinks in error messages, which means every compile error can have URL garbage leaking through.

